# =========================================================================================
# ca-params.env - the enclave's internal PKI.  Copy to ca-params.env and edit.
#
#     cp ca-params.env.example ca-params.env && chmod 600 ca-params.env
#
# Every value is written as ${VAR:-default}, so an environment variable set before the script
# runs WINS. A params file that clobbers an explicit override cannot be tested against a
# scratch directory - which is how this was found.
# =========================================================================================

# --- identity ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These appear in every certificate the enclave issues, and in the AO's evidence package.
CA_COUNTRY="${CA_COUNTRY:-US}"
CA_ORG="${CA_ORG:-McVey Consulting}"
CA_OU="${CA_OU:-Enclave PKI}"
CA_ROOT_CN="${CA_ROOT_CN:-Enclave Root CA}"
CA_ISSUING_CN="${CA_ISSUING_CN:-Enclave Issuing CA}"

# --- algorithms ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RSA-4096 / SHA-256 throughout. Both are FIPS 140-3 approved, so keys generated now under the
# default OpenSSL provider stay valid once `pro enable fips-updates` switches the provider at
# step 05 - nothing has to be reissued.
#
# ECDSA P-384 would be smaller and faster and is equally approved. RSA is chosen for maximum
# compatibility, because debugging a TLS handshake inside an air gap is expensive.
CA_KEY_BITS="${CA_KEY_BITS:-4096}"
CA_LEAF_KEY_BITS="${CA_LEAF_KEY_BITS:-3072}"
CA_DIGEST="${CA_DIGEST:-sha256}"

# --- lifetimes (days) ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# The root is offline and rarely used, so it is long. Leaves are one year to start: build the
# renewal automation BEFORE the first expiry rather than after the first outage.
CA_ROOT_DAYS="${CA_ROOT_DAYS:-3650}"
CA_ISSUING_DAYS="${CA_ISSUING_DAYS:-1826}"
CA_LEAF_DAYS="${CA_LEAF_DAYS:-365}"

# --- where the CA lives --------------------------------------------------------------------
# ROOT: on stage-01, OUTSIDE the enclave. Its private key must never be on an enclave machine.
# For production this belongs on removable encrypted media or a hardware token, and the AO will
# ask which. Recorded as a lab compromise, not a recommendation.
CA_ROOT_DIR="${CA_ROOT_DIR:-/srv/enclave-ca}"

# ISSUING: on svc-mgmt-01, the management plane. Its key is generated there and never leaves;
# only a CSR travels to the root for signing.
CA_ISSUING_DIR="${CA_ISSUING_DIR:-/etc/enclave-ca}"

# --- Revocation and name constraints -------------------------------------------------------
# All optional. All of them have to be decided BEFORE the certificates they affect are
# issued, because none of them can be added to a certificate that already exists.

# CRL distribution point, written into every leaf certificate at signing time.
#
# PLAIN HTTP, DELIBERATELY. Fetching a CRL over HTTPS from the same CA is circular: you would
# need the CRL to validate the certificate you are using to fetch the CRL. Serving CRLs over
# http is the standard resolution, and enable-tls.sh --redirect-http --crl <dir> keeps /crl/
# exempt from the 301 for exactly this reason.
#
# Leaving this empty means expiry-only revocation, which is the current documented position
# (runbook 2.9b). Setting it is a COMMITMENT: an expired CRL fails harder than no CRL, so
# gen-crl has to run on a schedule.
#CA_CRL_URL='http://svc-mgmt-01.enclave.internal/crl/enclave-issuing.crl'
#CA_CRL_DAYS='90'

# OCSP. Not recommended here: it needs a responder daemon, and `openssl ocsp` is a test tool
# rather than a service. Left as a parameter so the option is not closed off.
#CA_OCSP_URL=''

# nameConstraints on the ISSUING CA, applied by the root when the issuing certificate is
# signed. pathlen:0 already stops the issuing CA minting another CA; this stops it minting a
# certificate for a name the enclave does not own, so a stolen issuing key is useless against
# anything outside these names.
#
# Note the netmask form for IP - a CIDR suffix is not accepted here. And note that a name TYPE
# with no constraint is UNCONSTRAINED: permitting DNS and IP does not restrict email or URI
# names. Add excluded; clauses if the AO requires the stronger reading.
#
# Retrofitting means re-signing the issuing CA. Cheap while the enclave is small.
#CA_NAME_CONSTRAINTS='critical, permitted;DNS:.enclave.internal, permitted;IP:10.2.20.0/255.255.255.0'

# How many CAs may appear BELOW the issuing CA. 0 means it signs end-entity certificates and
# nothing else, which is the right default and the current decision (runbook 2.9c: Kubernetes
# self-signs its own three CAs and the enclave PKI does not chain into it).
#
# Set to 1 ONLY if the decision changes to chaining Kubernetes' kubernetes-ca / client-ca /
# front-proxy-ca under this authority. Note what that costs: Canonical Kubernetes takes the
# CA *keys* at bootstrap, so it puts a signing key on the control plane - a machine that runs
# workloads. It also does not remove self-signed certificates from the cluster, because etcd
# and the k8sd cluster certificate are self-signed regardless.
#
# FIXED AT SIGNING TIME. Changing it later means re-signing the issuing CA, which is the same
# event as applying CA_NAME_CONSTRAINTS - decide both together or pay for the event twice.
#CA_ISSUING_PATHLEN='0'
